add(14054,"This chapter develops the board representation, terminal rule, evaluation function and search algorithms used in the Caro project. The current frontend and its Worker adapter are examined alongside two research implementations: the modified Rust engine recorded in the 23 October source snapshot [6], and the separate native reference engine [8]. The active application calls a vendored WebAssembly package through the request interface and Worker adapter [3], [9], [26]. These sources have different roles: frontend source establishes request and commit behavior, Rust source explains the recorded algorithm, and binary execution establishes the behavior of the tested package.","Separates source ownership and binary execution; removes unsupported claim that old baseline plus adapter establishes active binary internals.",[app+'game/ai/AIController.ts',app+'game/wasm/executeSearch.ts',snapshot+'SOURCE_EXCERPTS.md',snapshot+'provenance.json'])
add(14743,"The repository also retains a TypeScript bot under src/game/bot. It is referred to as the legacy bot because AIController sends ordinary requests to GomokuWasmEngine and uses a separate tactical check for the four higher difficulty levels. Iterative deepening, quiescence search and transposition-table examples in this chapter come from that legacy implementation [3], [11]. Experiments on the separate Rust reference engine are identified as native reference results [8].","Removes generic promotion and keeps advanced algorithms assigned to legacy/reference owners.",[app+'game/ai/AIController.ts',app+'game/bot/search.ts',ref+'README.md'])
add(16290,"The formulations and pseudocode state the input, transition rule and assumptions of each algorithm. Worked examples illustrate their operation; measurements are presented separately in Chapter 5. Depth-limited search follows the distinction between look-ahead and static horizon evaluation described by Shannon [12].","Removes inflated presentation language and separates explanatory examples from measured results.",[snapshot+'SOURCE_EXCERPTS.md','https://doi.org/10.1080/14786445008521796'])
add(16758,"A position contains 225 cells, each empty, X or O. For a logical matrix B[y][x], row-major conversion is i = 15y + x, with inverse x = i mod 15 and y = floor(i/15). The center (7,7) therefore has index 112, and (14,14) has index 224. In the modified Rust source, Board stores a 15 × 15 array of repr(u8) cells and a move counter. Its cell values are Empty = 0, Black = 1 and White = 2; the array occupies 225 bytes, while the complete Board also contains the counter and any alignment padding [6], [13]. The frontend GameState instead stores occupied coordinates in a Map and maintains ordered move history [14]. The legacy BotBoard uses a one-dimensional array padded with boundary sentinels. These representations support different operations: direct Rust cell access, frontend match history, and incremental line analysis in the legacy bot.","Corrects frontend Map representation; removes false zero-per-node-allocation and unmeasured L1-cache claims; distinguishes cell array from struct size.",[engine+'board.rs:Board',app+'game/core/GameState.ts:GameState',app+'game/bot/board.ts:BotBoard','https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/type-layout.html'])
add(17988,"In the modified Rust snapshot, Board::place checks bounds, rejects an empty stone or occupied cell, writes the stone and increments move_count. Board::undo rejects an invalid or empty coordinate, then clears the occupied cell and decrements the counter [6]. These operations maintain the position. Turn alternation, move history and terminal lockout belong to the Rust Game wrapper and the frontend match controller, rather than to Board::place itself [2].","The checked undo and empty-stone rejection are modified-source behavior, absent from old baseline; describes legality owners without universal guarantee.",[engine+'board.rs:Board::place',engine+'board.rs:Board::undo',engine+'lib.rs:Game',app+'app/GameController.ts'])
add(18557,"Board mutation and match progression have separate owners. Board holds the cells and move count; Game and the frontend controller maintain turn, result and history. Search explores hypothetical moves on a working board, while the match commits only the selected move. The modified Rust search clones the root board once and applies and undoes moves within that copy [6]. This avoids a complete board copy for each child, although candidate generation still allocates move lists at internal nodes.","Clarifies actual root-copy/in-place ownership and counters earlier zero-allocation claim.",[engine+'search.rs:search',engine+'search.rs:SearchContext::value',engine+'candidates.rs:select_candidates'])
add(19301,"Source: Illustration of logical row-major indexing, based on the Rust board representation [6], [13].","GameState is a sparse Map, not the illustrated contiguous row-major Rust board.",[engine+'board.rs:Board',app+'game/core/GameState.ts'])
add(19823,"// Algorithm 2.1 (modified Rust source in [6]) — APPLY(B, (x,y), p):","Pseudocode includes checks not present in baseline_engine; correct provenance.",[snapshot+'SOURCE_EXCERPTS.md:W2'])
add(20418,"The try/finally structure in this explanatory pseudocode requires restoration after a return, cutoff or supported abort. An implementation must provide equivalent cleanup for every exit it supports. A matching apply/undo restores the cell and count in O(1) time; hashes, cached patterns, turn and other mutable fields must be restored separately by their owners. The modified Rust kernel uses structured returns without a deadline-abort path, whereas the legacy TypeScript search uses try/finally when it can throw a search-abort sentinel.","Avoids attributing exception/abort-safe finally semantics to Rust source; distinguishes O(1) cell updates from full state update costs.",[engine+'search.rs:SearchContext::value',app+'game/bot/search.ts:alphaBeta',app+'game/bot/board.ts:makeMove'])
add(21516,"For example, let X occupy (7,7), O occupy (8,7), and move_count be two. Applying X at (7,8) sets that cell and raises the count to three; undoing the same move restores both fields. Clearing the cell without decrementing the counter would make fullness checks unreliable. With direct indexing, each cell/count update takes O(1) time and O(1) auxiliary space; copying an N × N board takes O(N²). The archived native experiment records 820 successful working-board restoration checks, including repeated search configurations. This is the finite test result used in Chapter 5 [43].","Bounds restoration evidence to recorded checks; removes unlimited 'after every search' claim.",[engine+'board.rs',snapshot+'engine_verification.json',snapshot+'GATES.md'])
add(22285,"Source: Illustration of paired apply/undo in the modified Rust source [6].","Corrects baseline provenance.",[snapshot+'SOURCE_EXCERPTS.md:W2'])
add(22828,"The procedure receives a valid board and the last placed coordinate m. It returns the winning player and collected winning cells, or no result. It assumes the preceding position was unfinished, so any newly created winning line passes through m. An arbitrary imported position instead requires a full-board result check at the search root. Under the frontend state contract, an empty last-move coordinate yields no winner [4].","Clarifies valid-board precondition rather than asserting WinRule itself validates arbitrary invalid state.",[app+'game/core/WinRules.ts:FreestyleGomokuWinRule.evaluate',engine+'board.rs:check_winner'])
add(24998,"For X X [X] X X on a row, with the bracketed cell placed last, each side contributes two stones and 1 + 2 + 2 = 5. For X X X [X] X X, the run exceeds five and is still a win. A gap or board edge stops the corresponding scan. With K = 5, the frontend examines at most 4 × 2 × 4 = 32 neighboring positions, giving O(K) time and O(K) space for the collected cells. These bounds are constant for the configured winning length. A full-board scan checks every occupied starting point and has a different cost; the report does not infer an elapsed-time speedup from the operation count alone.","Removes unmeasured major-speedup claim and invalid exact comparison to 900 full-board checks; keeps derivation.",[app+'game/core/WinRules.ts:collect',engine+'board.rs:check_winner'])
add(26159,"A random baseline selects a legal move without comparing future replies. In the modified Rust snapshot, Easy first rejects an empty player or terminal board, enumerates empty cells, and selects an index using a seeded 64-bit mixer modulo the list length. It evaluates the chosen move afterwards to populate the result; that evaluation does not influence selection [6].","Assigns Random policy to modified source instead of old baseline.",[engine+'ai.rs:baseline',snapshot+'SOURCE_EXCERPTS.md:W4/W7'])
add(26873,"The seed is part of the input because the mixer is deterministic. The output is a member of L or no move. A board with one stone has 224 empty cells, while a one-element list always returns its sole coordinate. Terminal handling precedes modulo selection, so a full board cannot cause division by zero. Index selection takes O(1) after enumeration. Modulo reduction does not in general produce an exactly uniform distribution over arbitrary list lengths.","Corrects exact-uniformity implication and explains selection safely without marketing claims.",[engine+'ai.rs:seeded_u64',engine+'ai.rs:baseline'])
add(27880,"The modified Rust Normal policy selects an immediate win when one is available, then an immediate blocking coordinate, and otherwise the highest-valued one-ply candidate. Candidate assessment supplies the win and block markers [6]. The frontend has a separate tactical resolver for Hard, Very Hard, Expert and Master; Easy and Normal bypass it. A frontend tactical result identifies its source as tactical and reports zero Rust nodes, because it returns before a WASM request [3], [10].","Corrects source identity and keeps the two tactical stages distinct.",[engine+'ai.rs:baseline',app+'game/ai/AIController.ts',app+'game/ai/TacticalMoveResolver.ts'])
add(30777,"Source: Illustration of the modified Rust rule-based priorities [6].","Old [7] source is noise-based minimax, not shown Random/RuleBased dispatcher.",[snapshot+'SOURCE_EXCERPTS.md:W4/W7'])
add(31769,"The archived evaluator assigns weights of 1,000,000 to a run of at least five; 50,000 or 5,000 to a four with two or one open ends; 5,000 or 500 to a three; and 200 or 50 to a two. A run of five or more receives the same weight even with closed ends. Other cases receive zero [15]. These manually chosen weights favour longer, open formations, while the center term adds a smaller positional preference. They are heuristic parameters, rather than a proof that a named threat always outweighs every combination of shorter runs.","Removes false exponential progression, unsupported double-three modelling and unconditional tactical dominance.",[engine+'eval.rs:pattern_score',root+'/research/current_2026_10_23/baseline_engine/src/eval.rs:pattern_score'])
add(32635,"// Algorithm 2.5 (modified Rust evaluator in [6]; run weights in [15]) — EVALUATE(B, p):","Old baseline lacks clamp although weights match; cites correct implementation.",[snapshot+'SOURCE_EXCERPTS.md:W4',engine+'eval.rs:evaluate'])
add(33158,"After each visited run start, score contains the signed run contributions processed so far and the center contributions of previously visited occupied cells. The preceding-cell guard skips interior stones of the same run. Every maximal run therefore contributes once per axis, even when it contains several stones.","Removes duplicated invariant proof while preserving exact claim.",[engine+'eval.rs:evaluate'])
add(33685,"For a board containing only X at (7,7), no length-two-or-longer run contributes, and the center term gives E(B,X) = 14. Adding X at (8,7) creates an open horizontal two worth 200; the center terms are 14 and 13, giving 227. For a run shorter than five, changing an empty endpoint to an occupied endpoint changes its open-end classification and corresponding weight. A board boundary also closes an endpoint.","Corrects unsupported order-of-magnitude drop: two-stone weights differ by factor four, not ten.",[engine+'eval.rs:pattern_score',engine+'eval.rs:count_line'])
add(34154,"Scanning maximal runs in four directions takes O(N²) time and O(1) auxiliary space beyond the board: each occupied cell belongs to at most one maximal run per direction. This evaluator counts contiguous runs. A split formation such as XX_XX is represented as separate runs at the horizon, although candidate assessment can identify the empty gap as a winning completion. Thus horizon evaluation and tactical move assessment capture different properties of the position.","Removes repetitive benefits/trade-off template and unsupported implication deeper search always resolves split threat.",[engine+'eval.rs:evaluate',engine+'candidates.rs:assess'])
add(35028,"Source: Illustration of the run weights in the archived Rust evaluator [15].","Corrects [7] ai.rs citation to actual eval.rs weights [15].",[root+'/research/current_2026_10_23/baseline_engine/src/eval.rs:pattern_score'])
add(35143,"Terminal outcomes are checked before the depth cutoff. In the modified Rust snapshot, M = 100,000,000 and q is the ply distance from the root, capped at 225. A root win returns M − q, a root loss returns −M + q, and a draw returns zero. Nonterminal evaluations are clamped to the closed interval [−H,H], where H = M − 225 − 1 = 99,999,774 [6].","Corrects open-interval notation to inclusive clamp and specifies modified implementation.",[engine+'eval.rs:terminal_utility',engine+'eval.rs:HEURISTIC_LIMIT'])
add(38110,"Each appended coordinate is in bounds, empty and previously unseen. Conversely, every empty cell in a visited neighborhood is appended on its first encounter. This establishes membership in the radius policy. With m occupied cells, board scanning and neighborhood enumeration take O(N² + m(2r + 1)²) time before assessment; the seen matrix and move list use O(N²) space. The policy reduces the set searched, but can exclude legal moves outside the chosen neighborhood.","Removes arithmetically inconsistent 8–36/72.39–83.64% comparison and unmeasured tractability claim.",[engine+'board.rs:get_candidates'])
add(41005,"For k raw candidates, bounded directional assessment takes O(kK), sorting takes O(k log k), and move storage takes O(k), in addition to neighborhood generation. Let C be the raw set, T its assessed win-or-block subset, and c the normalized cap. Selection retains T plus the highest-ranked non-tactical moves needed to reach c, when available. Its total size is min(|C|, max(c, |T|)); it can exceed c. The separate native reference experiment in cap_loss.json illustrates the danger of a hard cap: cap 6 retained six of twenty candidates and omitted both winning endpoints. That result concerns the reference policy, while the modified policy preserves every assessed member of T [16].","Fixes cap semantics, bounds tactical guarantee and separates reference hard-cap failure from modified engine.",[engine+'candidates.rs:select_candidates',ref+'results/cap_loss.json'])
add(42751,"If A, B and C have quick scores 10, 50 and 30, score order is B, C, A, whereas coordinate order may be A, B, C. Ranking B first does not establish that it is the best searched move. Minimax still evaluates every retained child; alpha-beta may reach a useful cutoff sooner. Sorting k moves takes O(k log k), plus key computation and O(k) move storage in the described implementation. Whether the saved search work outweighs ranking cost is measured in Chapter 5.","Removes unsupported maximum-cutoff claim and unscoped benchmark percentages; keeps concrete example.",[engine+'candidates.rs:select_candidates',snapshot+'engine_search_summary.csv'])
add(44756,"Let U(s,R,q) be terminal utility at root distance q, and E(s,R) the nonterminal horizon evaluation. Transition T(s,a) places the side-to-move stone and changes the turn. For a fixed candidate function A and rule/evaluation configuration, the depth-limited value is","Makes ply-dependent terminal utility explicit and identifies turn in state.",[engine+'search.rs:SearchContext::value',engine+'eval.rs:terminal_utility'])
add(44924,"V(s,d,q) = U(s,R,q) if s is terminal;","Carries terminal ply explicitly in recurrence.",[engine+'eval.rs:terminal_utility'])
add(44958,"V(s,0,q) = E(s,R) if s is not terminal;","Consistent recurrence arguments.",[engine+'search.rs:SearchContext::value'])
add(44996,"V(s,d,q) = max[a∈A(s)] V(T(s,a),d−1,q+1) if MAX moves;","Consistent recurrence with terminal ply distance.",[engine+'search.rs:SearchContext::value'])
add(45045,"V(s,d,q) = min[a∈A(s)] V(T(s,a),d−1,q+1) if MIN moves.","Consistent recurrence with terminal ply distance.",[engine+'search.rs:SearchContext::value'])
add(45559,"    t ← TERMINAL_UTILITY(winner from WIN_AT(B,last), IS_FULL(B), root, ply)","WIN_AT returns player plus cells; terminal utility accepts winner, not tuple.",[app+'game/core/WinRules.ts',engine+'eval.rs:terminal_utility'])
add(46101,"The root routine validates an existing result, searches each retained root move, stores a best action and restores the board after each branch. Recursive checks use the just-played coordinate, because a newly formed line passes through that move. The modified Rust implementation uses one recursive kernel whose configuration enables plain minimax or alpha-beta pruning while retaining the same candidate policy and evaluator [6].","Assigns unified kernel to modified source and avoids vague active-engine identity.",[engine+'search.rs:SearchContext::run',engine+'search.rs:SearchContext::value'])
add(47585,"For branching factor at most b > 1 and depth d, the complete generated tree has O(b^d) nodes. Branching varies with position, candidate policy and terminal events. Depth-first search stores O(d) stack frames, up to O(db) live move entries, and an O(N²) working board. Candidate generation and horizon evaluation add per-node costs, so O(b^d) describes the node growth rather than a complete elapsed-time model.","Removes repeated benefit template and ambiguous 'exact game-theoretic' claim beyond finite heuristic tree.",[engine+'search.rs:SearchContext::value',engine+'candidates.rs:select_candidates'])
add(49025,"// Algorithm 2.11 (modified Rust search kernel in [6]) — ALPHABETA(B, player, root, depth, last, ply, alpha, beta):","Old baseline minimax differs from displayed unified fixed-depth kernel.",[snapshot+'SOURCE_EXCERPTS.md:W5/W6'])
add(49160,"    t ← TERMINAL_UTILITY(winner from WIN_AT(B,last), IS_FULL(B), root, ply)","Correct winner projection for terminal utility.",[engine+'eval.rs:terminal_utility',app+'game/core/WinRules.ts'])
add(50264,"In the preceding depth-two tree, searching A first returns 3 and raises the root alpha to 3. At B, MIN encounters 2 first and lowers beta to 2. Since beta ≤ alpha, MIN already has an option no greater than 2, so B cannot improve MAX's available value of 3. The remaining leaf, illustrated as 9, can be skipped. The root still selects A with value 3. Another traversal order may produce fewer cutoffs while retaining the same root value.","Corrects awkward plural 'choose the 2 branches' and makes bound argument precise.",['https://doi.org/10.1016/0004-3702(75)90019-3'])
add(50772,"Alpha and beta summarize bounds supplied by previously examined alternatives and ancestors. When they cross, the remaining siblings cannot improve the relevant ancestor's choice. Combined with the minimax and restoration invariants, this preserves the root value on the same finite tree [17]. The archived modified-engine corpus records 408 minimax/alpha-beta pairs, and the separate native reference corpus records 112 pairs with equal root scores. These totals include repeated configurations; they are finite experiment results, presented with their node counts in Chapter 5 [8], [41], [43].","Removes mixed unscoped pruning percentages and distinguishes repeated pair counts from independent positions.",[snapshot+'engine_verification.json',snapshot+'GATES.md',ref+'results/verification.json'])
add(54353,"The legacy TypeScript findBestMove uses a performance.now() deadline, checks it at the first search node and every 32 nodes, and catches a dedicated abort sentinel. It initializes a fallback candidate before an iteration completes and reports depth zero if the first iteration aborts. Recursive search uses try/finally to undo moves during unwinding [11]. The historical Rust reference instead uses a node budget, and its aggregate excludes work inside an aborted iteration [8]. The modified Rust engine uses the fixed tier depths shown in Table 2.1 [6]. These are separate budget and counter conventions.","Removes unmeasured reproducibility/per-tier time claims and distinguishes exact budgeting implementations.",[app+'game/bot/search.ts:checkAbort',app+'game/bot/search.ts:findBestMove',engine+'ai.rs:Difficulty::depth',ref+'README.md'])
add(55343,"Source: Illustration of completed-depth handling in the legacy TypeScript bot [11].","Caption previously cited unrelated native reference [8].",[app+'game/bot/search.ts:findBestMove'])
add(56447,"// Algorithm 2.13 (Zobrist scheme illustrated by the legacy board in [20]) — FULL_HASH(B, player):","FULL_HASH is explanatory pseudocode, not literal source symbol.",[app+'game/bot/board.ts:computeInitialState'])
add(56768,"For a four-bit example, let h = 1010 and the inserted stone key be 0110, temporarily omitting the side key. Applying XOR gives 1100; applying XOR with 0110 again restores 1010. The same identity applies to each half of the legacy bot's 64-bit fingerprint. XORing both the stone key and side key on make and undo restores the hash in O(1) time; restoring cached line counts has its own cost.","Avoids implying whole legacy move update is O(1) merely because hash update is.",[app+'game/bot/board.ts:makeMove',app+'game/bot/board.ts:undoMove'])
add(57588,"The legacy board stores the fingerprint as two 32-bit halves and uses a seeded table of stone and side keys. Make and undo also update pattern counts and stoneCount [20]. These fields must all return to their preceding values; the hash alone is not a complete restoration check. In the separate native reference, a winning move can leave to_move unchanged, so the side-key update follows that implementation's actual turn transition [8].","Plain academic prose and concrete owner semantics.",[app+'game/bot/board.ts:makeMove',app+'game/bot/board.ts:undoMove',local+'/caro-core-reference/src/state.rs',ref+'README.md'])
add(59871,"// Algorithm 2.14 (general TT contract; legacy implementation in [11]) — PROBE(key, required_depth, alpha, beta, context):","Generic context-validated pseudocode is stronger than actual legacy probe; do not present it as literal source behavior.",[app+'game/bot/search.ts:probeTT'])
add(60734,"The probe is sound only when the key, search context, score convention and depth interpretation are compatible. Checking depth, bound type and move legality alone does not establish all of these conditions. Classification uses the original search window, and storage and retrieval must agree about fail-hard or fail-soft returns. A conventional entry.depth ≥ required_depth test accepts deeper results; strict equality with a particular depth-limited horizon requires an additional depth-reuse contract.","Removes false guarantee that depth/bound/legality checks alone prevent corruption; clarifies deeper horizon semantics.",[app+'game/bot/search.ts:probeTT',app+'game/bot/search.ts:storeTT',ref+'results/cache_scope_probe.json'])
add(62579,"The legacy TypeScript table uses fixed-size parallel arrays. A slot is indexed from the low hash half, then both halves are compared before its depth, flag and score are considered. The stored move is used as an ordering hint [11]. The native reference instead uses a hash map [8]. These storage choices do not establish compatibility between search contexts. A hint must refer to a legal retained move before it influences move application, and cache lifetime must match the interpretation of its stored scores.","Removes speculative project advice while preserving actual array-vs-map distinction and necessary contract.",[app+'game/bot/search.ts:probeTT',app+'game/bot/search.ts:scoreMove',local+'/caro-core-reference/src/search/transposition.rs'])
add(63252,"Expected lookup is O(1) for a suitably configured hash table; actual access cost depends on indexing, collisions and replacement. Memory is proportional to allocated slots or entries, together with container overhead. A hash map's initial capacity is not a maximum, and the size of one entry is not the container's total allocation. A TT can save repeated search work and supply move hints, at the cost of memory and the need to preserve compatible score and state semantics.","Removes repetitive rhetorical template and symbol M conflict with terminal score; retains measurable complexity boundaries.",[app+'game/bot/search.ts:TT_SIZE',local+'/caro-core-reference/src/search/transposition.rs'])
add(63999,"Source: Illustration of bound classes and lookup in the legacy TypeScript search [11].","Corrects caption from unrelated [8] source to actual legacy search.",[app+'game/bot/search.ts:probeTT'])
add(68190,"The legacy TypeScript bot classifies a line segment with a bitmask: one denotes the player's stone and zero an empty cell, while an opposing stone ends the segment. Its evaluator precomputes categories for segment lengths up to 15 using tests for existing fives and empty cells that would complete a five. The categories are FIVE, OPEN_FOUR, FOUR, OPEN_THREE, BROKEN_THREE and NONE. They follow this classifier's completion-count rules and differ from the modified Rust evaluator's contiguous-run weights [20], [53].","Names exact legacy categories and avoids vague claim of identical strategic semantics.",[app+'game/bot/eval.ts:initPatternTable',app+'game/bot/board.ts'])
add(68749,"// Algorithm 2.16 (legacy pattern evaluator in [53]) — CLASSIFY_SEGMENT(mask, length):","Classifier lives in eval.ts, not board.ts; preserves existing reference mapping without fabricating source location.",[app+'game/bot/eval.ts:initPatternTable'])
add(70715,"The legacy evaluatePosition scans board lines and sums the category weights from the requested perspective. Other routines consult the incrementally maintained counts. With fixed-time pattern-table lookup per segment, a full scan over four axes takes O(N²) time; recomputing the four affected lines takes O(N) per player. The table is initialized for lengths up to 15, so this implementation requires that limit to be reviewed before using a larger board. Its cached counts remain consistent only when every move and undo follows the update procedure [20], [53].","Clarifies full evaluator vs cached counts and removes repetitive generic advice.",[app+'game/bot/eval.ts:evaluatePosition',app+'game/bot/eval.ts:MAX_LEN',app+'game/bot/board.ts:updateLines'])
add(71566,"The application offers local two-player mode and human-versus-bot mode. The latter has six labels: Easy, Normal, Hard, Very Hard, Expert and Master. Table 2.1 combines the frontend request path with the configurations in the modified Rust source and archived difficulty runs [3], [6], [9], [18]. These labels select policies and depths; no player study or strength rating is used to calibrate the spacing between tiers.","Removes unsupported progressive-skill claim and assigns table policy provenance correctly.",[engine+'ai.rs:Difficulty',app+'game/ai/contracts.ts',app+'game/wasm/contracts.ts',snapshot+'engine_difficulty_runs.csv'])
