add(73029,"The tiers change both search method and search width. Easy uses random selection; Normal adds immediate tactical priorities and one-ply evaluation. The remaining tiers use alpha-beta at depths 2–5 with radius 2 and nominal caps 15, 12, 10 and 8. Reducing the nominal width offsets some of the growth from increased depth, but these are soft caps: assessed wins and blocks can increase the retained set. For illustration, a uniform tree of width 8 and depth 5 has 8^5 = 32,768 leaves and 37,449 total nodes before pruning. This is a fixed-width model, not an upper bound for the implemented soft-cap search. The tier timings and actual counters are reported in Chapter 5; configuration alone does not establish a latency bound or a measured progression in playing strength.","Corrects leaves-vs-total nodes, false hard bounds, unmeasured 2000–5000 estimate, unsupported skill/UI guarantees and benchmark interpolation.",[engine+'ai.rs:Difficulty',engine+'candidates.rs:select_candidates',snapshot+'engine_difficulty_runs.csv'])
add(75156,"executeWasmSearch creates a Game, replays the request history through play_move, rejects an unsuccessful replay, calls ai_move, and frees the instance in finally. It converts Rust (x,y) to frontend (row,col). Its elapsed scope, worker-hydrate-and-search, includes reconstruction, search and cleanup, but excludes WASM loading and message delivery [27]. GomokuWasmEngine routes replies by Worker identity and envelope ID [26]. GameController separately checks its turn token, round, pause state, phase, mode and current player before accepting a move. These owners implement resource cleanup and stale-result rejection at different boundaries [2].","Corrects nonexistent direct requestId commit guard and exact elapsed timing scope; removes blanket deterministic guarantee.",[app+'game/wasm/executeSearch.ts:executeWasmSearch',app+'game/wasm/GomokuWasmEngine.ts:createWorker',app+'app/GameController.ts:startAiTurn',snapshot+'SOURCE_EXCERPTS.md'])
add(76626,"The current decision path uses frontend tactical checks and a Worker-hosted WASM request. The modified Rust source explains board updates, Freestyle detection, candidate generation, horizon evaluation and fixed-depth alpha-beta. The legacy bot supplies the iterative-deepening, hashing, transposition-table and quiescence examples. Their contracts are examined independently: candidate selection defines the searched tree; ordering changes its traversal; and alpha-beta preserves minimax's root value for that same tree under the stated assumptions.","Separates active path from legacy algorithms and removes unsupported unconditional 100%-threat and acceleration claims.",[app+'game/ai/AIController.ts',engine+'search.rs',app+'game/bot/search.ts'])
add(77248,"Chapter 5 evaluates the recorded implementations using native search experiments, WASM parity checks and browser Worker observations. Each result is interpreted with its own position, configuration, platform and counter definition. The theoretical arguments in this chapter establish properties of specified algorithms; the experiments assess those properties on the recorded finite fixtures [8], [43], [45], [46].","Replaces audit-style future instructions and vague multi-level-validation prose with concise academic synthesis.",[snapshot+'engine_verification.json',snapshot+'wasm_ts_parity.json',snapshot+'G4_real_browser_worker.json',ref+'results/verification.json'])
add(51580,"The worst case remains O(b^d) nodes. For a uniform tree with perfect ordering, the ideal minimum leaf count is b^ceil(d/2) + b^floor(d/2) − 1; for even depth this has the familiar O(b^(d/2)) growth [17]. Neither bound is an elapsed-time prediction, because node costs and branching vary. Memory remains depth-first: O(d) stack frames and up to O(db) live move entries, in addition to the board. Equality cutoffs preserve the root value, although tie selection and visited-node counts can depend on traversal order.","Makes odd-depth ideal leaf count precise and removes redundant generic disclaimer list.",["https://doi.org/10.1016/0004-3702(75)90019-3","https://webdocs.cs.ualberta.ca/~jonathan/publications/ai_publications/csn.pdf",engine+'search.rs'])
add(52880,"// Algorithm 2.12 (completed-iteration scheme illustrated by [11]) — ITERATIVE(B, maximum_depth, budget):","Generic contract omits literal fallback/sentinel details; identify it as explanatory rather than exact source listing.",[app+'game/bot/search.ts:findBestMove'])
add(61722,"The native reference records a context-reuse failure: after a clustered depth-4 search, same-root warm reuse returns the same score of 0, but after playing a move and changing the root player, a fresh table returns 0 and the reused table returns 19,800 [21]. The stored scores are root-relative, and the key omits that perspective as well as other configuration fields. This is a result from the reference engine. It motivates matching cache lifetime to the search context. Ply-relative mate scores also require normalization or a restricted reuse scope when stored values can be reached at a different root distance.","Keeps concrete negative reference result without repeating unsupported-active-engine disclaimers or a broad advisory list.",[ref+'results/cache_scope_probe.json',local+'/caro-core-reference/src/search/optimized.rs',local+'/caro-core-reference/src/zobrist.rs'])
add(64096,"A fixed-depth horizon can fall immediately before a forcing reply, causing a static evaluation to overrate the position. Quiescence search extends selected tactical continuations beyond that horizon. The legacy TypeScript bot implements this extension with a separate depth limit and forcing-move generator [11]. Its result describes that selective continuation tree.","Removes repeated verification language and states actual owner and scope directly.",[app+'game/bot/search.ts:quiescence'])
add(67292,"Stand-pat evaluation supplies the initial bound without placing a stone. Because Gomoku requires a move, this is a modelling assumption rather than a legal pass; an immediate cutoff in a forced-defense position need not equal an all-legal-replies result. The forcing set and the five-level extension limit define the selective horizon. With at most f > 1 forcing moves per level and q extra levels, a coarse bound is O(f^q) nodes and O(qf) stored move entries, plus per-node generation, ordering and evaluation costs. If f = 1, node growth is O(q).","Retains substantive theoretical limitation, corrects f=1 edge case and removes vague call for more verification.",[app+'game/bot/search.ts:quiescence'])
add(74625,"AIController receives the application state, round identity and selected difficulty, then assigns a request identifier. Easy and Normal proceed directly to GomokuWasmEngine. The four higher tiers first call the TypeScript tactical resolver for O; a returned move becomes a tactical result without Rust search. Otherwise the controller copies move history into the engine request. This frontend prepass and the Rust Normal rule-based policy are separate decision stages [3], [10].","Corrects source citation for tier routing: AIController is [3], resolver geometry is [10].",[app+'game/ai/AIController.ts',app+'game/ai/TacticalMoveResolver.ts'])
add(69498,"For the five-cell segment XX_XX, setting the middle zero creates five consecutive bits, giving one winning completion and the FOUR category. A segment with two distinct winning completion cells is OPEN_FOUR if no five is already present. The category is computed for the whole segment, with the precedence in Algorithm 2.16; an opposing stone or board boundary ends that segment [53].","Gives exact category for worked example and cites actual classifier source.",[app+'game/bot/eval.ts:initPatternTable',app+'game/bot/eval.ts:evaluateLine'])
